{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica","id":17325,"title":"Public Relations: Mexica","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-24T14:59:16-08:00","id":17325,"title":"Public Relations: Mexica","content":"Rereading some of the old books, it seems like each pantheons has 'friends' amongst the other pantheons <i>except the Aztlanti</i>. Which got me thinking: which of the pantheons do the Aztlanti have the best relationship with? Or is it all 'nobody likes these guys, but nobody can prove they're titanspawn; sort of things?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I used to know things. Forbidden things.</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","name":"Brasstreader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar306_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T15:19:04-08:00","id":17333,"title":"","content":"I'd assume they'd have relatively good relations with the Psedjet, and fought some of the same Titans alongside the Annunaki (Tlaltecuhtli = Tiamat)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T15:28:15-08:00","id":17338,"title":"","content":"The Japanese don't particularly get along with anyone either, so it isn't like the Aztec are alone in their alone-ness. It's not just that the Aztec Pantheon is blood thirsty (so are the Irish and Norse) but also that the Aztecs are pretty xenophobic. They didn't much care for outsiders. Or anyone that wasn't a model citizen according to their social rules.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T15:39:14-08:00","id":17344,"title":"","content":"Deities that required daily human sacrifices probably aren't typically seen as &quot;good&quot; to people in this modern era despite what those sacrifices mean.<br /> <br /> However, I would assume any pantheon with a deity that had such sacrifices to a degree to be more understanding. And there are plenty of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T16:09:41-08:00","id":17359,"title":"","content":"Go back far enough and pretty much all religions had human sacrifice. Fun stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T16:14:31-08:00","id":17362,"title":"","content":"Too much of a generalization for me.<br /> <br /> Archaeologists are still skeptical that it was as prevalent as some authors wrote of, such as Rome and what it said of Carthage, and then of the Gaulish Druids.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T20:19:11-08:00","id":17478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=17362#post17362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Too much of a generalization for me.<br /> <br /> Archaeologists are still skeptical that it was as prevalent as some authors wrote of, such as Rome and what it said of Carthage, and then of the Gaulish Druids.</div> </div> </div> </div>The generalization for the most part is absolutely true. What was, however, stretched and exaggerated was the amount of sacrifice that each religion other than the one that an author ethnocentrically-preferred performed. The Spanish monk who spoke of the Aztecs claimed one such ritual sacrificed 84,000 people for an annual event.... probably more like 80 if it was huge and dramtic, realistically maybe like ten or so.<br /> <br /> Norse Sacrifices! You did not want to be a dude and get invited to Uppsala's ultimate event, let me tell you. Nine males of every living creature were sacrificed about every nine years. If you were Christian, you bought off the Jarls or you were probably one of the guests of honor. Not to mention at the end of a Jarl's life it was expected to send along a slave girl or two with him ...and lemme tell you, they didn't just hang out on that burning boat drinking wine and diving overboard when the fire caught up to them.<br /> <br /> Celt Sacrifice! They did not go all out as the Roman Propaganda machine depicted, but there is evidence that indicates that human sacrifice had occurred. Headless corpses, bodies discovered with evidence buried around sacred sites, and the whole idea of headhunting alone was a strange enough idea.<br /> <br /> Greeks and Romans! While the Greeks may or may not have had human sacrifice, the Romans most certainly performed it when they were still closely tied to the Estrucan religion. One of the theories about the bloody gladiator games was that it was an evolution from earlier fights. In fact, human sacrifice needed to be banned in Rome.<br /> <br /> And that's just the big three of Europe. China, Japan; the ancient cultures in the Levant and along the northern coast of Africa; African religions, Carthage does have evidence of child sacrifice though probably not to the extent the Romans professed.<br /> <br /> Shoot, even Christianity has one MAJOR event of human sacrifice, and that's Jesus dying on the cross. It was supposed to be so massive that it wiped out the earlier need to offer lambs and goats.<br /> Human Sacrifice has probably been around since we first learned how to kill other people and started believing in all-powerful deities that controlled aspects of the world. We're a species bound by habit and if you killed a person and suddenly rain flooded over your drought-plagued fields, you'd probably just continue doing it every drought to come just in case<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T08:48:23-08:00","id":17737,"title":"","content":"In my game, I always had Tlaloc and Sobek rather chummy. They had similar daily roles and rather monstrous appearances. I had a storyline where the two thought that it would be funny to swap places, but it all went wrong. Tlaloc refused to bring the rains without <i>some</i> amount of sacrifice and Sobek had no idea what to do with all the blood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1011-phgraves","name":"PHGraves","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1011_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T10:42:22-08:00","id":17799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=17359#post17359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Go back far enough and pretty much all religions had human sacrifice. Fun stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>While a fair point, few religions took it to the extremes that the Celtic and Aztec ones did. Headhunting aside, we have historical records that indicate Celts strung the entrails of their enemies between trees as a way of celebrating the victory.<br /> <br /> The Aztecs apparently needed man-made rivers for all of the blood they could shed following a victory in battle. Note that this is the post-battle celebration.<br /> <br /> What makes them different is that the Celts didn't spend their time in direct conflict with every other pantheon they came across. Heck, there are two (or three, if you're counting the Aesir) &quot;distinct&quot; pantheons just in Scion that can be considered Celtic. Plus their mythology clearly indicates contact with other pantheons and heroes that isn't violent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T10:53:29-08:00","id":17808,"title":"","content":"That's true enough. When you combine the Aztec interest in just <i>bunches</i> of dead people and their racism, you have a very unfriendly group of deities. The Japanese Gods are pretty racist, but far less murderous. The Celtic Gods are pretty murderous, but not all that racist (by ancient standards). <br /> <br /> It doesn't require much demonization of the Aztec society, which Scion is still guilty of, to explain why they're not on good terms with anyone but themselves. That part is fairly accurate. They just weren't an outgoing, friend-making type of Pantheon. The fact that they kinda freak out a bunch of the less blood-loving ones is just an extra reason why the other Pantheons don't <i>care</i> about how unfriendly the Aztecs are.<br /> <br /> Maybe GriffinGuy24 will come along and tell us how very wrong we are and that the Aztecs are super friendly and totally would have awesome relationships with Pantheon X... but I don't really think so. There are some that might have a better relationship with than others, but they just don't strike me as having BFFs like the Greeks and Egyptians do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T16:15:44-08:00","id":18000,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=17808#post17808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe GriffinGuy24 will come along and tell us how very wrong we are and that the Aztecs are super friendly and totally would have awesome relationships with Pantheon X... but I don't really think so. There are some that might have a better relationship with than others, but they just don't strike me as having BFFs like the Greeks and Egyptians do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, this IS in my wheelhouse...<br /> <br /> First of all, I don't think there're ANY two pantheons who you could unironically call BFFs. At best, they're political allies. The Greeks and Egyptians may have had good relationships, but also incredibly poor ones. Frenemy is a better way to put it.<br /> <br /> I'd say that the Aztecs, Maya, and Inca all share similar enough backgrounds and histories that, while they are all extremely different civilizations and pantheons, they can probably agree on enough and understand each other well enough that, if the conditions were right, the three of them would be a devestating alliance. Friction between them is a near certainty, particularly Aztec-Maya, but if pressed, and ESPECIALLY if there's a common enemy, they'd be a force to be reckoned with. One thing to note about those two other pantheons is that the Maya and the Inca both were sacrificing people at the same time the Aztecs were, though not in the same way or on the same scale. So, they're less likely to flip out about the blood.<br /> <br /> For the most part though, I hope that Scion 2e doesn't demonize the Aztecs the way they were in Scion 1e. Plenty of pop culture already does that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T17:51:37-08:00","id":18051,"title":"","content":"Well, one thing that can be said that is certainly positive, is that the Aztecs had a well-functioning society that I believe was fairly prosperous...and <i>thriving</i>. Tenochtitlan was huge, and with a population of around a quarter million was one of the greatest cities of that timeframe in the world. (So, perhaps the gods were doing something right...) And then there's Nezahualcoyotl, one of the greatest rulers (Tlatoani) in the region.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T18:38:56-08:00","id":18076,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18051#post18051\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, one thing that can be said that is certainly positive, is that the Aztecs had a well-functioning society that I believe was fairly prosperous...and <i>thriving</i>. Tenochtitlan was huge, and with a population of around a quarter million was one of the greatest cities of that timeframe in the world. (So, perhaps the gods were doing something right...) And then there's Nezahualcoyotl, one of the greatest rulers (Tlatoani) in the region.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not to mention they were one of the few civilizations at the time to have compulsory education for all citizens. For most other societies at the time, compulsory education was strictly for children of royalty and nobility.<br /> <br /> But about the subject at hand, yeah, there needs to be a LOT less Aztec demonization in 2e, and more detail abot the Atzlanti's realtions with other pantheons. Maybe they still aren't &quot;friends&quot; with other pantheons per se, but I'd at least like to see which ones they might have teeth-clenched teamwork with.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/990-maplesamurai","name":"MapleSamurai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar990_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T20:39:54-08:00","id":18132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18000#post18000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, this IS in my wheelhouse...<br /> <br /> First of all, I don't think there're ANY two pantheons who you could unironically call BFFs. At best, they're political allies. The Greeks and Egyptians may have had good relationships, but also incredibly poor ones. Frenemy is a better way to put it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's really, really hard to draw lines between the Tuatha and the Gallic and the Skots pantheons. Primarily because they share roughly 75% of their total number of deities between them. There's frankly not a whole lot of modern history for the Celtic pantheons because they converted to Christianity very quickly by comparison to much of the world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18000#post18000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say that the Aztecs, Maya, and Inca all share similar enough backgrounds and histories that, while they are all extremely different civilizations and pantheons, they can probably agree on enough and understand each other well enough that, if the conditions were right, the three of them would be a devestating alliance.</div> </div> </div> </div>Are you sure this is actually your wheelhouse? Do you know <i>anything</i> about Central and Southern American history pre-Conquista?<br /> <br /> Not only do the Aztec, Mayans, and Inca not share pantheons, they don't even share a significant number of gods. Considering that the Mayans alone had nearly 150 gods this is pretty damning to the premise that there were more than facile similarities. Oh, hey, sky gods. Just like literally every other religion <i>ever</i> (although Abrahamic monotheist religions call them Archangels).<br /> <br /> Frankly, the amount of cultural drift present in these three major religious/cultural groups has fascinated anthropologists for over a century now. The amount of drift has forced scientists to re-estimate the original settlement of the Americas.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18000#post18000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Friction between them is a near certainty, particularly Aztec-Maya, but if pressed, and ESPECIALLY if there's a common enemy, they'd be a force to be reckoned with. One thing to note about those two other pantheons is that the Maya and the Inca both were sacrificing people at the same time the Aztecs were, though not in the same way or on the same scale. So, they're less likely to flip out about the blood.<br /> <br /> For the most part though, I hope that Scion 2e doesn't demonize the Aztecs the way they were in Scion 1e. Plenty of pop culture already does that</div> </div> </div> </div>Ritual sacrifice was a part of all three cultures, yes, although it was far less pronounced than amongst the Aztec. I agree that we could do with less demonization of the Aztec. An adequate explanation for why they have no real allies is that their extreme xenophobia -- arguably justified considering what happened to their civilization -- prevents them from functioning as members of a collective organization beyond the classic &quot;I won't kill you until this is over&quot; alliance of desperation.<br /> <br /> Frankly, we just don't have any great examples of the Aztec working with other pantheons in recorded history and we have several catalyzing events that would ensure their original overt hatred for non-Aztec continued as xenophobia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T23:05:44-08:00","id":18202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18132#post18132\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are you sure this is actually your wheelhouse? Do you know <i>anything</i> about Central and Southern American history pre-Conquista?</div> </div> </div> </div>I cannot help but notice that the majority of your post seems to be written as an argument against things that GriffinGuy24 did not actually say. For example, you say <i>&quot;Not only did they not share pantheons&quot;</i>, when GriffinGuy24 said <i>&quot;they are all extremely different civilizations and pantheons&quot;</i>. You say <i>&quot;damning to the premise that there were more than facile similarities.&quot;</i>, when GriffinGuy24 did not include any such premise. This trend continues throughout your post.<br /> <br /> Perhaps you are used to defending the Mexica people from arm-chair mythologists, and made some assumptions about his post which he did not make? It is an admirable motivation, but probably misplaced in this case. At the very least I can assure you that GriffinGuy24 earned his living digging around in mesoamerican ruins.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":484,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","user":"Iry","date":". 11-26-2013, 02:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T23:21:44-08:00","id":18213,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18202#post18202\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I cannot help but notice that the majority of your post seems to be written as an argument against things that GriffinGuy24 did not actually say. For example, you say <i>&quot;Not only did they not share pantheons&quot;</i>, when GriffinGuy24 said <i>&quot;they are all extremely different civilizations and pantheons&quot;</i>. You say <i>&quot;damning to the premise that there were more than facile similarities.&quot;</i>, when GriffinGuy24 did not include any such premise. This trend continues throughout your post.<br /> <br /> Perhaps you are used to defending the Mexica people from arm-chair mythologists, and made some assumptions about his post which he did not make? It is an admirable motivation, but probably misplaced in this case. At the very least I can assure you that GriffinGuy24 earned his living digging around in mesoamerican ruins.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. Apologies to Griffinguy24. I didn't mean to attack you, I was merely misunderstanding your intent. I grew up around an inordinate amount of archaeologists and anthropologists who specialize in early American civilizations and know just enough to get myself in trouble.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-25T23:27:25-08:00","id":18217,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18213#post18213\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fair enough. Apologies to Griffinguy24. I didn't mean to attack you, I was merely misunderstanding your intent. I grew up around an inordinate amount of archaeologists and anthropologists who specialize in early American civilizations and know just enough to get myself in trouble.</div> </div> </div> </div>No harm done! Also, I want to take a moment to award you an internet for being so damned awesome. Apologies of any kind are essentially an endangered species online. *salute*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T17:13:00-08:00","id":18821,"title":"","content":"Apology accepted. I can't say that I'd not do the same thing in your shoes once Intellect-Extremity rage mode sets in.<br /> <br /> My main point was to say that under the right circumstances, those three pantheons have enough in common that they could easily form a powerful alliance. They're also as fractious and forceful enough that they could easily dissolve into infighting that could break the southern Americas apart. But I mistakenly neglected to mention that getting these folks in the same room had the potential to cause serious damage, because I thought the context of &quot;the Aztec gods seem to have no friends&quot; was already spoken for in the premise of this thread. I should have clarified<br /> <br /> But yeah, the Aztecs and the Incas both had enormous, expansionist empires built around taking over areas and either converting the populace to their religion (for the Incas) or using them as sacrifice (Aztecs). Even though the empires never encountered one another, the pantheons are going to be giving each other significant looks. And the Maya knew and got into conflicts with the Aztecs historically, so there's plenty of bad blood there to potentially wreck things. And individual gods have the potential to ruin everything forever (I'm looking at you, Tezcatlipoca and Pachacamac)<br /> <br /> However, in addition to the potential for mayhem, there's also the potential for comraderie. The importance of blood sacrifice, as well as the gods' maintenance of the balance of the cosmos and the need for human worshippers to bolster it, are common ground, as is the shared history of subjugation under the Spanish (So, if any European pantheons start messing with one of the three, the other two might decide to act before they get the shaft themselves). And, of course, never underestimate the ability of Scions to throw their own monkey wrenches into this.<br /> <br /> The point isn't that the three are the same (hells no) or a ready-made alliance, but that if you asked me which pantheons could be a potential ally to the Aztecs (and I was), I'd put them on the short list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T18:58:22-08:00","id":18871,"title":"","content":"Well, until I read up on the Maya and Inca fan-splats (and the rest of my players do, too) I need to figure out which of the ten Developer Pantheons would be most likely to form an alliance with the Aztecs. I'm using the Air Titan as the major antagonist; he's manifesting storms all throughout the world in order to destroy international connections between Scion groups. The players are using Mictlan as a go-around, but in order to go beyond North and South America, the Aztlanti are trying to gain access to the other pantheon's Underworlds. If pushed to desperation, they're willing to cement the alliance with Quetzalcoatl acting as a go-between for a while (the other pantheons seem to hate him less, for some odd reason).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I used to know things. Forbidden things.</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","name":"Brasstreader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar306_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T20:21:24-08:00","id":18912,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18821#post18821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, in addition to the potential for mayhem, there's also the potential for comraderie. The importance of blood sacrifice, as well as the gods' maintenance of the balance of the cosmos and the need for human worshippers to bolster it, are common ground, as is the shared history of subjugation under the Spanish (So, if any European pantheons start messing with one of the three, the other two might decide to act before they get the shaft themselves). And, of course, never underestimate the ability of Scions to throw their own monkey wrenches into this.<br /> <br /> The point isn't that the three are the same (hells no) or a ready-made alliance, but that if you asked me which pantheons could be a potential ally to the Aztecs (and I was), I'd put them on the short list.</div> </div> </div> </div>But by this standard they'd be on the short list to be friendly with the Aesir (human sacrifice of willing Norsemen at Uppsala was a major part of the religion until it was forced underground by Christianity) and the Celts, who practiced far more consistent human sacrifice of a variety that directly parallels the Aztec tradition in intent if not blood spilled.<br /> <br /> Is it really problematic that a pantheon or two have no real allies or capability to maintain long-term alliances? It certainly motivates them to have strong Scions and adopt people with the potential to be strong Scions. It also gives their Scions good reason to want to interact with the Scions of other pantheons. While their parents might not be interested in reaching out, children tend to be far more willing to ask for help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T21:12:50-08:00","id":18932,"title":"","content":"Hey, I'm totally fine with a pantheon not having many or any allies. Most gods don't treat other religious beliefs with much respect. However, I do think that any relationship between pantheons that isn't explicitly stated in mythology (Like Perun having to be restrained to prevent him from going to fight Thor, or just the fact that the Deva and Yazatas think the others are demons) is up to an individual ST as to their relationship. <br /> <br /> And if someone wants to do so, I think the Aesir and the Celts could be potential allies, or bitter enemies, your choice, to the Aztecs. They share a great deal of warrior-culture, value of brave actions sort of deal, enough that they might do well fighting Titans together. Then again, their cultures are such that they could frequently butt heads and do as much damage to each other as the Titans could. There are a number of possibilities here.<br /> <br /> ... Also, I have played with several people who have extensive knowledge of Celtic myth. And I have NEVER had any indication that their human sacrifice was a parallel to the Aztecs' intent, since Aztec human sacrifice was primarily meant to empower the gods and provide stability to the cosmos, thus preventing an apocalypse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T03:44:54-08:00","id":19073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=18932#post18932\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... Also, I have played with several people who have extensive knowledge of Celtic myth. And I have NEVER had any indication that their human sacrifice was a parallel to the Aztecs' intent, since Aztec human sacrifice was primarily meant to empower the gods and provide stability to the cosmos, thus preventing an apocalypse.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, Celtic mythology -- the original, actual mythology vice the Wiccan rewrite -- has very strong parallels to the Norse mythology, with a world tree under threat from firbolg (giants), dragons (Jormungandr), traitors (left undefined -- for the Irish it's strongly hinted that it may be Manannan, the elder god who is not like the other Tuatha, e.g. immortal heroes), et cetera.<br /> <br /> While the stringing of entrails was meant as an offering to The Morrigan in thanks for victory in battle (Celtic warriors appealed to The Morrigan before battle and in battle for strength and victory), there was also &quot;straight up&quot; human sacrifice to appease the gods.<br /> <br /> There's been a lot of whitewashing of Celtic and Germanic pre-Christian religions to make them more palatable to the modern stomach in the neo-paganist movements. While I don't advocate human sacrifice, I think sweeping that sort of history under the rug is, at best, disingenuous. It certainly leads to people believing they understand the depth and breadth of the original practice without truly realizing what was involved.<br /> <br /> &quot;Happier and lighter&quot; in the perspective of the Bronze Age means &quot;you aren't going to be killed for no particular reason.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":889,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","user":"Dayen","date":". 11-27-2013, 06:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T05:15:03-08:00","id":19103,"title":"","content":"While that is interesting to know, the picture you're painting of Celtic sacrificial practices seems to indicate that they were primarily made to honour or appease the Gods. The Aztec mindset holds blood sacrifice necessary to the continuation of the cosmos, by powering the Gods in their daily battles to keep the universe running. Big difference. If anything, from what I understand of the Aztec mindset (though feel free to correct me if I'm wrong), they might very well disapprove of wasting Blood on simple veneration of the Gods unnecessarily; they, after all, actually use the Blood to power themselves. The Celtic Gods do not even have that capability, lacking the power of Itztli. All they would see is wasteful killing of the people ruled by a Pantheon, and their Duty and Loyalty Virtues are bound to revolt. (I apologize for rambling a bit. English is not my first language, so I hope I got my point across).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T07:09:26-08:00","id":19153,"title":"","content":"~~Hmf..to speak of Celtic sacrifice is to speak of the druids and their religious beliefs, and let's face it, no one knows much that is certain about Celts and human sacrifice. Some authors say yes, others say no. <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_sacrifice#Celts\" target=\"_blank\">Probably both are correct</a>. Some tribes did, while others didn't.<br /> Just saying that it's too much a generalization. Similar to the one that was made when it was claimed that Native Americans tortured and cannibalized their enemies.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T13:58:00-08:00","id":19340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19073#post19073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, Celtic mythology -- the original, actual mythology vice the Wiccan rewrite -- has very strong parallels to the Norse mythology, with a world tree under threat from firbolg (giants), dragons (Jormungandr), traitors (left undefined -- for the Irish it's strongly hinted that it may be Manannan, the elder god who is not like the other Tuatha, e.g. immortal heroes), et cetera.<br /> <br /> While the stringing of entrails was meant as an offering to The Morrigan in thanks for victory in battle (Celtic warriors appealed to The Morrigan before battle and in battle for strength and victory), there was also &quot;straight up&quot; human sacrifice to appease the gods.<br /> <br /> There's been a lot of whitewashing of Celtic and Germanic pre-Christian religions to make them more palatable to the modern stomach in the neo-paganist movements. While I don't advocate human sacrifice, I think sweeping that sort of history under the rug is, at best, disingenuous. It certainly leads to people believing they understand the depth and breadth of the original practice without truly realizing what was involved.<br /> <br /> &quot;Happier and lighter&quot; in the perspective of the Bronze Age means &quot;you aren't going to be killed for no particular reason.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Once again, you are responding to things I did not actually say. I never mentioned any neopagan influences in any context. I assume that you're referring to my mention of gaming with people who &quot;have extensive knowledge of Celtic myth&quot; and automatically assumed I was talking about Wicca/neopaganists. I wasn't. I was talking about people, many of whom post on this forum, who care about the various Celtic myths the way I care about Mesoamerican ones. <br /> <br /> You also never addressed what I actually said about the differences between Aztec and Celtic human sacrifices. Yes, the Celts and Norse both did post-battle celebratory sacrifices. This is true, though probably not to the extent that sources like the Romans claimed. However, the Aztecs didn't do this to appease the gods. They did it so that the gods would be empowered and thus more able to do their job of keeping the world running smoothly. They thought that if they failed to provide sacrifices, the sun wouldn't rise, the rains would stop, the crops would fail, and monsters from the stars would come down and eat their flesh. Does THAT match the intent behind Celtic and Norse sacrifice? Cause from what I know, it doesn't","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T17:50:05-08:00","id":19461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19340#post19340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You also never addressed what I actually said about the differences between Aztec and Celtic human sacrifices. Yes, the Celts and Norse both did post-battle celebratory sacrifices. This is true, though probably not to the extent that sources like the Romans claimed. However, the Aztecs didn't do this to appease the gods. They did it so that the gods would be empowered and thus more able to do their job of keeping the world running smoothly. They thought that if they failed to provide sacrifices, the sun wouldn't rise, the rains would stop, the crops would fail, and monsters from the stars would come down and eat their flesh. Does THAT match the intent behind Celtic and Norse sacrifice? Cause from what I know, it doesn't</div> </div> </div> </div>As an actual Irish Reconstructionist Polytheist, I can back you up on this. While there may have been some human sacrifices in pre-christian Irish religion, most info on what we have points otherwise. Animal Sacrfice, yes, but human seems rather rare; unless you count head-hunting as human sacrificingng, which I do not. <br /> <br /> But in the celtic thought, Offerings are about respect and hospitality to the gods, not about empowering them to do their duty. I provide offerings to them out of respect, hospitality and gratitude but not to make them stronger. The idea that if we do not give offerings that the sun will stop rising is not really part of celtic belief.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1381-herkles","name":"herkles","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T20:50:38-08:00","id":19528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19340#post19340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You also never addressed what I actually said about the differences between Aztec and Celtic human sacrifices. Yes, the Celts and Norse both did post-battle celebratory sacrifices. This is true, though probably not to the extent that sources like the Romans claimed. However, the Aztecs didn't do this to appease the gods. They did it so that the gods would be empowered and thus more able to do their job of keeping the world running smoothly. They thought that if they failed to provide sacrifices, the sun wouldn't rise, the rains would stop, the crops would fail, and monsters from the stars would come down and eat their flesh. Does THAT match the intent behind Celtic and Norse sacrifice? Cause from what I know, it doesn't</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm skeptical of the headhunting claim, as that is primarily from Roman sources.Whereas stringing the entrails is primary sourced from the &quot;Irish histories,&quot; and while I don't discount the value of propaganda that doesn't seem like the sort of thing you claim of your own culture lightly. Brian Boru's brother Ulf strings the entrails of his Norseman foe between the trees being the example most poignant in my mind.<br /> <br /> I agree that for the most part Celtic offerings are meant as a thank you to the gods, not a &quot;it's dangerous to go alone, take this!&quot; moment.<br /> <br /> It's harder to say with the Norse because the Eddas don't really touch on the behavior of the godi, just the duties of gods and men and everything in between to prevent Ragnarok from being the end of everything. It's not exactly clear why the Aesir would have wanted willing Norse sacrifices (or unwilling Christian ones as later was the case at blots), but considering everything the Aesir did and do was/is to either stave off or prepare for apocalypse, that's a fair parallel in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-28T00:03:43-08:00","id":19587,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19528#post19528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm skeptical of the headhunting claim, as that is primarily from Roman sources.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alright. No. We have a fair number of Irish sources that reinforce the idea of headhunting being a part of their culture. The Destruction of Da Derga's Hostel has repeated mentions of heads in this context. Such as the repeated line &quot;for thou wilt carry off the head of the king of another country&quot;<span style=\"font-family:arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">. In the same myth we have </span>Lomna Dr\u00fath who has his head cut off and thrown about to fulfill a prophecy. Conaire Mor has his head cut off in this same myth, and after it has been removed, it remains alive to converse.<br /> <br /> Then,moving on to The Cattle Raid, we have &quot;two sons of Nera son of Nuathar son of Tacan... [who] vied which of the twain would be the first to fight and contend with Cuchulain, which of them would inflict the first wound upon him and be the first to behead him.&quot; and then, because they are trying to fight Cuchulain, they lose, and he &quot;turned on them, and straightway he struck off their four heads from themselves Eirr and Indell and from Foich and Fochlam, their drivers, and he fixed a head of each man of them on each of the prongs of the pole.&quot;<br /> <br /> There are tons of more beheading in the Cattle Raid, I don't feel like continuing to list them is going to do much asides from prove that in that myth headhunting was predominant.<br /> <br /> But, on with more myths. Conall Cerach, Cuchulain's foster brother has a lot of head involving myths, such as the construction of the &quot;Brain ball&quot;, and in the Coir Anmann we have this prophecy regarding him &quot;Never will be born a boy who will be more impious than this boy towards the Connacians; and not a night will he be without a Connaughtman's head on his belt&quot;. Further proof of a presence of headhunting.<br /> <br /> And then in a big get together (The Feast of Bricriu), the Ulster heroes, including our friend Conall there, and Cuchulain, which is, again, filled with head related things. We have &quot;Cu Chulainn [springing] upon [his enemies], so that the nine of them fell dead to the earth. He heaped their heads in disorder into the seat of watching and resumed his post. Another nine shouted at him in like manner he killed three nines, making one cairn of them, heads and accouterments.&quot; and later in the myth, we have what is probably the inspiration for Gawain and the Green Knight, being a back and forth involving a deal &quot;to cut off your head tonight, [and] you to avenge it tomorrow night.&quot;<br /> <br /> Lastly, at least lastly in the number of things I'll mention here, we have Dian Cecht who kills his son Miach by cutting into his head, and eventually managing to successfully damage his son's brain. Rendering him impossible to revive.<br /> <br /> All of this is strong, non-Roman evidence that the Irish Celts in the least had a focus on headhunting in their culture.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19528#post19528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whereas stringing the entrails is primary sourced from the &quot;Irish histories,&quot; and while I don't discount the value of propaganda that doesn't seem like the sort of thing you claim of your own culture lightly.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually have no recolection of this sort of sacrifice being used by the Irish, or the other Celts of the Isles. And I'm fairly sure I don't remember the Romans rambling on about that. What &quot;Irish Histories&quot; are these?<br /> <br /> And, also. Reading back on your prior statements of...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19528#post19528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, Celtic mythology -- the original, actual mythology vice the Wiccan rewrite -- has very strong parallels to the Norse mythology, with a world tree under threat from firbolg (giants), dragons (Jormungandr), traitors (left undefined -- for the Irish it's strongly hinted that it may be Manannan, the elder god who is not like the other Tuatha, e.g. immortal heroes), et cetera.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know of zero world trees in Celtic Mythology. At the very least, not in Irish mythology. The closest thing I can think of, which is an incredibly long shot and doesn't really have anything to do with the idea of a true 'world tree' is the Druidic focus on the oak. But, we know nothing more then the fact that there was a focus since the Druids get the award for being more mysterious then most Mystery Cults.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, I know of zero refrences to the size of the firbolg (or that they are doing anything to any sort of tree), or any connection with the Jotun of Norse mythology, zero references to Dragons, asides from the Welsh dragons, but those are more Authorian then Celtic. Clearly influenced by Christianity, who likely brought dragons with them when they arrived.<br /> <br /> And, lastly, I have no clue where it is hinted that Manannan is a traitor. Manannan raises Lugh, doesn't kill Cuchulain when he has been sleeping with Fand, his wife, and a whole mess of other things. Manannan mac Lir is probably one of the most reasonable figures in Irish myth next to, say, Nuada.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19528#post19528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the stringing of entrails was meant as an offering to The Morrigan in thanks for victory in battle</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, I have never in my life heard of this being related to The Morrigan. Could you point me in the right direction?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":".  Forgot to mention the lack of Furbolg interaction with trees. 11-28-2013, 03:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T08:05:39-08:00","id":20642,"title":"","content":"Wolf/Rolf/Ulf's act of stringing up the entrails is specified in the Njals saga. Chapter 11, I believe. Only one I can remember at the moment, anyway. This occurred following the Battle of Clontarf, which I believe mentions Ulf;s victory and the offering in the primary sources, but I'm not at home and thus can't cite anything directly.<br /> <br /> Manannan is reliable, a favored arbiter, but he's also an outsider. He's an elder god by the consideration of the Tuatha. Not because he's an older druid, but because he's an older something else entirely. There's a difference between being considered a fair arbiter and being trusted unconditionally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T09:09:00-08:00","id":20658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=20642#post20642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wolf/Rolf/Ulf's act of stringing up the entrails is specified in the Njals saga. Chapter 11, I believe. Only one I can remember at the moment, anyway. This occurred following the Battle of Clontarf, which I believe mentions Ulf;s victory and the offering in the primary sources, but I'm not at home and thus can't cite anything directly.<br /> <br /> Manannan is reliable, a favored arbiter, but he's also an outsider. He's an elder god by the consideration of the Tuatha. Not because he's an older druid, but because he's an older something else entirely. There's a difference between being considered a fair arbiter and being trusted unconditionally.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't quite comment on the entrails bit save to mention that a Icelandic saga detailing entrail-stringing doesn't exactly make it an Irish offering to the Morrigan. <br /> <br /> As for Manannan mac Lir, he was trusted to foster children, gave Lugh the four gifcts of the four gifts to battle back the Formorians, and is the god to lead the Tuatha underground after their final defeat. He's known as a protector god of the Manx and honored pretty much in Welsh, Manx, and Irish religion. He might not be a part of the Tuatha, but he is not exactly an outsider as Loki was, or a traitor as Loki became.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T10:04:54-08:00","id":20675,"title":"","content":"The Battle of Clontarf was many years after the coming of Christianity and the subsequent conversion of the island. I would be incredibly surprised if there were sacrifices to The Morrigan as late as 1014. Not because I think that there wouldn't still be some people un-converted, but because of the social backlash against those people if they were to preform this ritual in anywhere public. I've flitted through Njals Saga just now on Project Gutenberg, and I can't find any reference to the sacrifice. Chapter 11 doesn't detail it unfortunately. I would be very interested in seeing this.<br /> <br /> And, as Aynie said, there is nothing that makes Manannan a bad, or possibly treacherous figure. And I would argue that he is, in fact, trusted more then the vast majority of the Tuatha would trust one and other. Seeing as they have the habit of killing members of their Pantheon quite frequently. Manannan's leading them underhill when the Milesians come is an example of this. I would have to say that, next to Nuada, there probably isn't a figure who the Tuatha will trust as much. The Dagda seduced Nuada's wife, Ogma's grandsons killed Lugh's father, Lugh had Ogma's grandsons killed, Aengus tricks The Dagda, Aengus beheads Lugh, Dian Cecht is just spiteful (no offence Herkles. But, dude's not very nice mythologically), The Morrigan kills Lugh's son, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Manannan's distance shouldn't be used to cast him as a potential traitor. That doesn't make much sense. Especially because he is a Death God, and they have a habit of being distant from their Pantheons. Donn is stuck off the coast of Dublin, Hades doesn't participate, Mictlantecuhtli doesn't leave Michtlan, Hel is in Helheim, so on and so forth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T11:55:21-08:00","id":20716,"title":"","content":"There is an irony here in that this discussion started when Dayen accused me of not understanding the differences between the Aztec, Maya, and Inca religions. Now, here he is claiming similarities between the Norse and Celtic myths where they don't exist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T12:38:25-08:00","id":20727,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=20675#post20675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Battle of Clontarf was many years after the coming of Christianity and the subsequent conversion of the island. I would be incredibly surprised if there were sacrifices to The Morrigan as late as 1014. Not because I think that there wouldn't still be some people un-converted, but because of the social backlash against those people if they were to preform this ritual in anywhere public. I've flitted through Njals Saga just now on Project Gutenberg, and I can't find any reference to the sacrifice. Chapter 11 doesn't detail it unfortunately. I would be very interested in seeing this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also that is about is one of the sagas of the Icelanders, not a native Irish account as well. But yea, by that time Ireland had long been christianized. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=20675#post20675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, as Aynie said, there is nothing that makes Manannan a bad, or possibly treacherous figure. And I would argue that he is, in fact, trusted more then the vast majority of the Tuatha would trust one and other. Seeing as they have the habit of killing members of their Pantheon quite frequently. Manannan's leading them underhill when the Milesians come is an example of this. I would have to say that, next to Nuada, there probably isn't a figure who the Tuatha will trust as much. The Dagda seduced Nuada's wife, Ogma's grandsons killed Lugh's father, Lugh had Ogma's grandsons killed, Aengus tricks The Dagda, Aengus beheads Lugh, Dian Cecht is just spiteful (no offence Herkles. But, dude's not very nice mythologically), The Morrigan kills Lugh's son, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Manannan's distance shouldn't be used to cast him as a potential traitor. That doesn't make much sense. Especially because he is a Death God, and they have a habit of being distant from their Pantheons. Donn is stuck off the coast of Dublin, Hades doesn't participate, Mictlantecuhtli doesn't leave Michtlan, Hel is in Helheim, so on and so forth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Manannan is a bit of an outsider, but that doesn't make him a traitor figure. He helps the gods, not just in finding a new home after the Milisian invasion, but also provided them, pigs that could be killed and come back to life and his cloak of Invisibility. Also as you say, he is a death God; he helps ferry people across the great ocean to the next life. <br /> <br /> As for him being trusted over the others, Lugh and Nuada are the ones that are the wise kings. Yes, there is Infighting, But I will disagree that they don't trust one another to some degree anyways. The Gods and Goddesses are not perfect, all good beings. I do not pretend that they are nor do I claim that they are. They are what they are after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1381-herkles","name":"herkles","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T12:46:34-08:00","id":20735,"title":"","content":"Oh yes, entirely Herkles. The Dia do, in all likelihood, trust the members of their Tuath. What I ment was that they probably trust Manannan in his council, and in general, more then one and other (With exception to their good kings). Not just because of their slights against one and other, but because Manannan doesn't ever do anything to earn anything but their gratitude. He kills Donn when asked to when the other death God insulted Danu, he is a wealth of magical things, Birthrights in Scion's mechanics, and is at his core an incredibly good person. By ancient standards, at least.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":".  Clarification. 11-30-2013, 03:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T23:01:24-08:00","id":21013,"title":"","content":"So, I found the referenced line in Njal's Saga full.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Then they threw a ring round Brodir and his men, and threw </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">branches of trees upon them, and so Brodir was taken alive.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Wolf the Quarrelsome cut open his belly, and led him round and </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">round the trunk of a tree, and so wound all his entrails out of </span><span style=\"color:#000000\">him, and he did not die before they were all drawn out of him.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">Brodir's men were slain to a man.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\">After that they took King Brian's body and laid it out. The king's head had grown fast to the trunk.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\"><br /> <br /> If you want more information on the connections drawn between the Germanic and Celtic traditions with regards to the axis mundi, you'll need to find a copy of Proinias MacCana's <i>Celtic Mythology</i>, Miranda Green's <i>The Gods of (the?) Celts</i> and <i>Celtic Dictionary of Myth and Legend</i>. You'd probably also do well for a copy of Hilda R. Ellis Davidson's <i>Myths and Symbols in Pagan Europe</i>. Most of the early German Celtic ritual sites were built around a centered pillar, not in any way dissimilar from the Norse tradition. Later sites added four perimeter pillars which I'm sure religious scholars are still arguing about the meaning of.<br /> <br /> As far as publicly performing rituals to honor old gods...you do realize that is basically the </span>modus conversionis<span style=\"color:#000000\"> of the Catholic Church, yes? I really shouldn't have to explain how every modern Christian holiday is a re-purposed pagan holiday because it was so difficult to make people stop their habit. It became necessary to anoint these things as &quot;Christian,&quot; even if they are clearly not.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T23:05:08-08:00","id":21015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21013#post21013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I found the referenced line in Njal's Saga full.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> If you want more information on the connections drawn between the Germanic and Celtic traditions with regards to the axis mundi, you'll need to find a copy of Proinias MacCana's <i>Celtic Mythology</i>, Miranda Green's <i>The Gods of (the?) Celts</i> and <i>Celtic Dictionary of Myth and Legend</i>. You'd probably also do well for a copy of Hilda R. Ellis Davidson's <i>Myths and Symbols in Pagan Europe</i>. Most of the early German Celtic ritual sites were built around a centered pillar, not in any way dissimilar from the Norse tradition. Later sites added four perimeter pillars which I'm sure religious scholars are still arguing about the meaning of.<br /> <br /> As far as publicly performing rituals to honor old gods...you do realize that is basically the [/COLOR]modus conversionis<span style=\"color:#000000\"> of the Catholic Church, yes? I really shouldn't have to explain how every modern Christian holiday is a re-purposed pagan holiday because it was so difficult to make people stop their habit. It became necessary to anoint these things as &quot;Christian,&quot; even if they are clearly not.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Mainland Celtic tradition is not Welsh or Irish Celtic tradition. Germanic Celtic rituals were probably similar because of the closeness. Irish and Welsh mythologies do not resemble Norse mythology because by the time the Vikings were raiding Ireland, Ireland had already been mostly converted to Christianity so the ties there would have been only through books and tales told to the raiders that had already had several centuries of Christian rule to dilute their origins.<br /> <br /> Also, that passage you've cited mentioned neither crows or the Morrigan so I'm confused at the tie there.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T23:23:55-08:00","id":21019,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aynie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21015#post21015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Mainland Celtic tradition is not Welsh or Irish Celtic tradition. Germanic Celtic rituals were probably similar because of the closeness. Irish and Welsh mythologies do not resemble Norse mythology because by the time the Vikings were raiding Ireland, Ireland had already been mostly converted to Christianity so the ties there would have been only through books and tales told to the raiders that had already had several centuries of Christian rule to dilute their origins.<br /> <br /> Also, that passage you've cited mentioned neither crows or the Morrigan so I'm confused at the tie there.</div> </div> </div> </div>You appear to be very confused. The question at the moment was whether the passage existed within the text. That question was answered. I'm plodding through my books looking for the connections between the offering of entrails and The Morrigan. Besides, you know, the obvious result of stringing entrails in a tree at a battlefield.<br /> <br /> As far as whether or not the world tree is present within Celtic mythology, frankly I'm fairly satisfied that it is given the scholarly work that draws direct parallels that exists.<br /> <br /> Of course, it isn't at all surprising that Indo-European religions would share symbolism given that they all owe significant chunks of their mythology to the PIE religion. Cross-pollination is the lazy answer. Root similarity is the far more supported theory, from a scholarly perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T23:41:39-08:00","id":21025,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=19073#post19073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> While the stringing of entrails was meant as an offering to The Morrigan in thanks for victory in battle (Celtic warriors appealed to The Morrigan before battle and in battle for strength and victory), there was also &quot;straight up&quot; human sacrifice to appease the gods.</div> </div> </div> </div>If by confused, you mean that I'm curious as to where your citations for such an affair being a sacrifice to the Morrigan came from, then sure, let's go with that. You never answered the question, after all. All that passage proves is that they eviscerated a guy and did so by hanging his guts in the tree. There's no mention of sacrifice beyond what you've personally inferred from the text. Now, if you can prove me wrong, I'd be happy to retract my claim. You said it was an offering to the Morrigan, I'm asking for literal proof or at least a proper source; not interpretation of a text that basically talks about a grisly way to murder someone. It doesn't even reference sacrifice in that passage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T23:43:02-08:00","id":21026,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21013#post21013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as publicly performing rituals to honor old gods...you do realize that is basically the [/COLOR]modus conversionis<span style=\"color:#000000\"> of the Catholic Church, yes? I really shouldn't have to explain how every modern Christian holiday is a re-purposed pagan holiday because it was so difficult to make people stop their habit. It became necessary to anoint these things as &quot;Christian,&quot; even if they are clearly not.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I entirely do know this. Please do not speak to me as if I were a fool. By the year 1014, the Irish had been heavily converted, to the extent that the Celtic Catholic Church would soon pose to be the chief rival of Roman Catholicism, and be the largest thorn in Pope Gregory the Converter's side for many years. Ireland was a seat of power for Christianity, and, while yes, their holy days are all picked up from here or there to make conversion of local populaces less difficult, human sacrifice wouldn't be tolerated. If you have any evidence to the contrary, that the Celtic Church would allow human sacrifice, let me know.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21013#post21013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You appear to be very confused. The question at the moment was whether the passage existed within the text. That question was answered.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, answered by being provided, but it has nothing to do with The Morrigan. There is no refrence in that quotation to why it is being preformed. And, furthermore, since that form of sacrifice was more common with the Germanic peoples then the Irish, I would suggest it was not a genuine Celtic sacrifice, but one that was brought over with the Norse invasions. Assuming, of course, that it was done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21013#post21013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as whether or not the world tree is present within Celtic mythology, frankly I'm fairly satisfied that it is given the scholarly work that draws direct parallels that exists.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is zero refrence to World Trees in Irish-Celtic mythology. Maybe in Iberian, Germanic, or Gaulish. But, nothing in Irish-Celtic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21013#post21013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, it isn't at all surprising that Indo-European religions would share symbolism given that they all owe significant chunks of their mythology to the PIE religion. Cross-pollination is the lazy answer. Root similarity is the far more supported theory, from a scholarly perspective.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, entirely. There are many common symbols through the Indo-European cultures, and many smiliar Deities, sociatal practices, and so on. But, I must refute any ideas that the Firbolg were assaulting a Irish-Celtic (since they were the only Celts who had them) World Tree from the simple lack of evidence in their mythology. The idea that Manannan is some sort of traitor figure makes zero sense, and is needlessly drawing paralells between himself and Loki. If you want a traitor, go to Bres. Not the lord of their best afterlife, the God who gives the Tuatha the majority of their potent items, protects his people on the Isle of Man, doesn't kill Cuchulain for sleeping with his wife, and leads the Tuatha when there are none left. There is zero evidence in this that he was seen as a traitor.<br /> <br /> Having a piece of scholarly work that backs up a theory is not that difficult. And, since you have made so many prior baseless claims, such as the sources for Celtic Headhunting being primarily Roman sources, I must disagree with you vehemently. I do not see a point in continuing this argument, since it is off topic (the thread is about the Aztecs), and because it is obvious that neither of us is going to convince the other.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T12:01:41-08:00","id":21810,"title":"","content":"Okay..<br /> So the Celts would be the pantheon most willing to let the Aztecs pass through their Overworld and/or Underworld while they attempt to remedy the Sky Titan's complete blockade of international air traffic? Is that the general premise so far?<br /> Not to take away the literature presented by any parties here, but I need to read more in both mythologies before I can supply any valid arguments on a scholastic level here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I used to know things. Forbidden things.</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","name":"Brasstreader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar306_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T12:27:20-08:00","id":21829,"title":"","content":"Frankly, the Aztecs are on roughly the same footing with any of the pantheons. As fun as it is to argue via scholarly texts, it comes down to the Storyteller. Ignoring the fact that there are no texts showing that the Aztec gods interacted with any others, any such those texts would be ancient past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1011-phgraves","name":"PHGraves","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1011_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T12:30:46-08:00","id":21831,"title":"","content":"The Irish Celts probably wouldn't be too bothered by the Aztecs. The sacrifices probably wouldn't bother them too much, if at all. But, actually, the Aztecs would probably view the Irish as fairly dodgy. If there was going to be any problems diplomatically between the two Pantheons, it would probably be the Aztecs taking offence to something the Irish did or have done. Either violating the incredibly strict Aztec laws regarding sex and sexuality, or from the Aztecs not having as central a concept of one's social face.<br /> <br /> For the sake of the game, just don't let The Morrigan, or The Dagda around when Tlazolteotl is around, and the Irish shouldn't care if the Teotl are poking around in Tir na nOg, or Tech Duinn. The Irish have a lot of hospitality laws, and because of that, should be fairly welcoming as long as the Teotl are not breaking things while they wander on through.<br /> <br /> On second thought, both Irish Death Gods (Donn and Manannan) don't belong to the Pantheon proper, so the Teotl would have to make seperate deals with the two of them. Nuada (or whichever King you have ruling in your game) can't call the shots in either of the Death God's lands.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 12-02-2013, 03:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T13:19:35-08:00","id":21855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=21831#post21831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Irish Celts probably wouldn't be too bothered by the Aztecs. The sacrifices probably wouldn't bother them too much, if at all. But, actually, the Aztecs would probably view the Irish as fairly dodgy. If there was going to be any problems diplomatically between the two Pantheons, it would probably be the Aztecs taking offence to something the Irish did or have done. Either violating the incredibly strict Aztec laws regarding sex and sexuality, or from the Aztecs not having as central a concept of one's social face.<br /> <br /> For the sake of the game, just don't let The Morrigan, or The Dagda around when Tlazolteotl is around, and the Irish shouldn't care if the Teotl are poking around in Tir na nOg, or Tech Duinn. The Irish have a lot of hospitality laws, and because of that, should be fairly welcoming as long as the Teotl are not breaking things while they wander on through.<br /> <br /> On second thought, both Irish Death Gods (Donn and Manannan) don't belong to the Pantheon proper, so the Teotl would have to make seperate deals with the two of them. Nuada (or whichever King you have ruling in your game) can't call the shots in either of the Death God's lands.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly what things would the Tuatha do that the Aztecs would find offensive?<br /> <br /> I just discovered the Gotham by Night Scion page and I was reading about Tlazolteotl and how she enforced the Aztecs strict laws about sex and sexuality. What are those laws, exactly? What did the Aztecs consider unacceptable practices?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T16:24:07-08:00","id":21966,"title":"","content":"And this would be more acceptable than, say, the Aztecs cutting through Mount Olympus/Gaia or Iteru/Akenaten on their way to the World? At least until the Sky Titan is reined in a little?<br /> <br /> Would the Japanese pantheon be adverse to a bunch of Aztecs trimming their way through Mikaboshi's forces on the path to ... well, the Aztecs need a major international airport to launch their coatls from, why not Tokyo?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I used to know things. Forbidden things.</b>","edited":{"userid":306,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","user":"Brasstreader","date":". 12-02-2013, 07:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","name":"Brasstreader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar306_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T17:07:18-08:00","id":21982,"title":"","content":"Griff is going to pop in and answer your question Unnatural. We have just sat down for a game, so it'll probably be some time tomorrow.<br /> <br /> And Brasstreader, yeah, it'd be more acceptable. The Theoi are probably going to be incredibly racist to everyone who's not Greek, and the human sacrifice would get a colossal amount of issue from them. Even their God of foreigners, Dionysus, would be at odds with the Teotl because of many of the laws the Aztecs have regarding the things he does, and how his general behavior would be utterly demonized in Aztec culture. And, actually, most of the Theoi would fall under those laws. So, cutting through Theoi lands would probably be a bad idea.<br /> <br /> And the Kami are super xenophobic as well. Generally when I have seen them in game, they are worried about Japan, and that's it. They don't like other people on their islands for any reason. The Aztecs would be one of the utterly worst in their minds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 12-02-2013, 08:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T19:43:08-08:00","id":22068,"title":"","content":"Basically, the Aztecs were among the most prudish cultures imaginable.<br /> <br /> This was largely because they were a culture dedicated to the common good above all else. Anything that wasn't explicitly done to contribute to society as a whole was considered wasteful. This applied to sex. In the eyes of Aztec law, sex that did not result in procreation was outlawed, and thus, homosexuality was punishable by death (impalement for active male homosexuals, dismemberment for passive males, garrotte for lesbians). Rape was a capital offense. Adultery was also highly frowned upon, though the Aztecs being just as sexist as almost everyone, it was much stricter for women than men (A married man could sleep with an unmarried woman, but if a married woman slept with a man, both parties were killed), though divorce was possible. Things like drunkenness and gambling were also seen as highly immoral. It should be noted that while these were all illegal and seen as wrong, actual enforcement of these laws wasn't all that common (though there were famous cases like the Texcocoan king Nezhualpilli had his son and the married woman he slept with killed, or the Tlaxcalan general who was killed along with his mistress).<br /> <br /> Of course, we're dealing with gods, not mortals, and enforcement is certainly a thing, and Tlazolteotl is the goddess of enforcing sexual mores, tempting people into breaking them, and punishing transgressors, typically with venereal diseases or Justicey things. Any non-procreative sex or adultery or rape is going to make her go into full on slut-shaming mode, and the Greeks are rife with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T19:54:19-08:00","id":22074,"title":"","content":"I see. So Tlazolteotl's temptation of people basically amount to her trying to get you to have sex with her?<br /> <br /> If you're a married man and you sleep with her? You get punished?<br /> If you're a woman and you sleep with her at all? You get punished?<br /> If you're an unmarried man you MAY be okay?<br /> <br /> Basically the idea of Tlazolteotl is that she's the one goddess in the Overworld you should absolutely NEVER have sex with?<br /> <br /> Do I have that right? I've been thinking a lot about the Teotl lately and I've been trying to decide how to better use them in my games, so figuring out how best to deal with Tlazolteotl would be nice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-03T05:04:45-08:00","id":22270,"title":"","content":"This is kinda odd. I knew the Teotl cared more for the upkeep of rituals and sacrifice, for how the cosmology worked than, say, the personal list of greatness that is Arete, but my group ended up interpreting that as the Aztlanti being sort of uninterested in aspects of sex, gambling, and whatnot. It was sort of like 'you can go out whoring and drinking with the Scions and the Moon Rabbits, whatever, just be in the shrine by 9:00pm sharp for the daily bloodletting or we'll be Severely Pissed.&quot; This notion that sexual codes existed wasn't known. 'As long as the sun rises'. Tlazolteotl was the one the party members confessed to when they were late, or did something incredibly stupid and needed to do something as recompense. She was like our camp counselor, not some blackmail-obsessed honey trap like Isis was.<br /> Of course, it was usually ol' Smoky Mirror (we never called him by his 'name-title', which made him gourchy) who came up with whatever punishment I needed to do. Which were never pleasant.<br /> Again, I should invest in some literature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I used to know things. Forbidden things.</b>","edited":{"userid":306,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","user":"Brasstreader","date":". 12-03-2013, 08:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/306-brasstreader","name":"Brasstreader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar306_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-04T21:49:25-08:00","id":23521,"title":"","content":"I should have probably responded sooner, but eh, life.<br /> <br /> RE: unnatural1 - If you're an unmarried man, there's a chance you can pray to Tlazolteotl for repentence... except, Tlazolteotl is married. To Xilonen, an incredibly minor Aztec maize deity (there's about a dozen of so of those). So yeah, sleep with Tlazolteotl, and you're fucked in every sense of the word. <br /> <br /> RE: Brasstreader - I'm not gonna say you're wrong for playing that way. STs can adapt the setting as they want, and it could be possible that the Teotl changed post-Conquista. Maybe not having an entire civilization granting them the necessary blood to fuel the cosmos made them rethink their priorities. Maybe (in your setting) the Aztecs overstated things. Maybe Tlazolteotl is trying to overcome some terrible Fatebindings. It doesn't necessarily need explaining.<br /> <br /> And Tezcatlipoca's greatest joy in life is the suffering of others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-05T05:38:14-08:00","id":23699,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>griffinguy24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/17325-public-relations-mexica?p=23521#post23521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And Tezcatlipoca's greatest joy in life is the suffering of others.</div> </div> </div> </div>